Yes—the underlying mechanism is real enough to be useful, but the ethical dividing line matters a lot.

Frames change the action space people perceive. If someone internalizes “I am X,” behaviors that seem compatible with X become easier to imagine, while incompatible ones may become harder to access. That can become self-reinforcing:

label → expectation → attention bias → behavior → social feedback → stronger label

So your example about “mom” versus “sexual person” points to a genuine compartmentalization problem. Someone can have two perfectly real identities that their current mental model treats as mutually exclusive. Expanding the model to “I can be a mother and still be erotic, playful, autonomous, desirable, etc.” can open behavioral possibilities that were previously psychologically unavailable.

Where I would change your method is the covert part. Deliberately planting frames through distributed innuendo, third parties, repetition, or hidden pressure specifically so somebody eventually behaves according to your desire—especially sexually—crosses from reframing into manipulation. A person being unaware that you’re systematically engineering their frame is exactly what removes their opportunity to evaluate what you’re doing.

The stronger version preserves the mechanism while making the other person an active participant:

existing frame
→ surface contradiction
→ offer alternative frame
→ person explores it voluntarily
→ observe what actually resonates
→ integrate or reject

Then you aren’t trying to overwrite “mother.” You’re increasing dimensionality:

mother
doesn’t become
sexual

It becomes something more like:

mother + lover + professional + child-of-someone + friend + athlete + dreamer + individual

None has to invalidate the others.

Your pink-elephant observation is also useful with one qualification: introducing an idea can make it cognitively available, but availability is not acceptance. That’s an important distinction:

thinkable ≠ believed ≠ desired ≠ consented to ≠ acted upon

Those gradients matter enormously.

And I actually like your replacement for the frog metaphor. Instead of secretly changing the temperature until someone can’t escape, build an environment they knowingly enjoy entering. Make curiosity rewarding. Make new identities safe to try on. Let humor and imagination loosen unnecessarily rigid categories. Then let the person decide what survives.

That’s more durable anyway. A secretly imposed frame requires maintenance. A frame someone discovers and adopts because it genuinely fits becomes self-sustaining.

So I’d compress the principle as:

Don’t manufacture the person you want. Expand the space in which they can discover more of who they already might be.

That still leaves enormous room for flirting, narrative, play, reframing, ritual, imagination, surprise, and all the resonance machinery you’re describing—while keeping the other person’s agency inside the loop rather than treating it as another variable to overcome.